WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.335 --> 00:00:02.135
Welcome to Luke Fish.

2
00:00:02.155 --> 00:00:05.790
In this lesson, a chess game becomes a map of Java.

3
00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:10.407
We will follow one move from the board, through the
rules, into the computer's thinking,

4
00:00:10.427 --> 00:00:12.187
and back to the screen.

5
00:00:12.207 --> 00:00:15.454
You do not need to understand every line at once.

6
00:00:16.043 --> 00:00:20.273
If you have written a little JavaScript, many ideas
will feel familiar.

7
00:00:20.293 --> 00:00:24.088
Java adds explicit types, classes, and compilation.

8
00:00:24.108 --> 00:00:27.588
We will connect each idea to real code in this
repository.

9
00:00:27.608 --> 00:00:32.058
Pause whenever you want to inspect a method or try
an experiment yourself.

10
00:00:32.668 --> 00:00:35.223
This is the actual Swing application.

11
00:00:35.243 --> 00:00:39.568
The board accepts clicks or a coordinate move such
as E two, E four.

12
00:00:39.588 --> 00:00:42.438
The Engine lab changes the opponent's settings.

13
00:00:42.458 --> 00:00:44.973
Pause lets you explore both sides.

14
00:00:44.993 --> 00:00:47.828
Analyze suggests a move without playing it.

15
00:00:48.418 --> 00:00:53.073
The thinking panel reports depth, visited positions
called nodes, and the predicted best

16
00:00:53.093 --> 00:00:56.410
continuation, called the principal variation.

17
00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.580
Displayed scores favor White.

18
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:02.870
Positive one point zero means roughly a pawn of
advantage, not a guaranteed win or

19
00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:04.465
a probability.

20
00:01:05.085 --> 00:01:08.060
A package groups related classes under a name.

21
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.550
The model package describes chess: pieces, moves,
positions, and game history.

22
00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:16.785
It does not need Swing to decide whether a move is
legal.

23
00:01:16.805 --> 00:01:19.955
That separation makes the rules easier to test.

24
00:01:20.543 --> 00:01:24.488
The engine package evaluates positions and searches
for a good move.

25
00:01:24.508 --> 00:01:27.768
The U I package draws the board and handles
interaction.

26
00:01:27.788 --> 00:01:30.388
Chess Frame coordinates these parts.

27
00:01:30.408 --> 00:01:32.823
Imports let one class use another.

28
00:01:32.843 --> 00:01:36.453
Notice the design principle: drawing a piece and
choosing a move are different

29
00:01:36.473 --> 00:01:38.348
responsibilities.

30
00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.800
Java source files compile into bytecode before the
Java virtual machine runs them.

31
00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.250
The J D K includes the compiler.

32
00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:51.630
In Eclipse, import the existing project and run
chess dot Main as a Java application.

33
00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:55.670
This project does not require Maven or an external
chess engine.

34
00:01:56.293 --> 00:01:58.233
Main is the entry point.

35
00:01:58.253 --> 00:02:03.030
Public makes it accessible, static means the method
belongs to the class, and void means

36
00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:04.960
it returns no value.

37
00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:08.630
String brackets describes an array of command line
arguments.

38
00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:12.690
The final block schedules window creation on Swing's
event thread.

39
00:02:13.293 --> 00:02:15.773
A class defines a kind of object.

40
00:02:15.793 --> 00:02:20.393
Calling new Game creates an instance, with its own
position and history lists.

41
00:02:20.413 --> 00:02:23.713
A constructor has the class's name and no return
type.

42
00:02:23.733 --> 00:02:28.613
Here, the no argument constructor delegates to
another constructor using this.

43
00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:32.955
Private fields keep implementation details inside
the class.

44
00:02:32.975 --> 00:02:37.350
Other code should use methods such as play and undo
to maintain the game's rules.

45
00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:39.260
This is encapsulation.

46
00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.632
Final on the class prevents inheritance; final on a
field prevents reassigning that field.

47
00:02:44.652 --> 00:02:48.447
These are related restrictions, not identical
meanings.

48
00:02:49.043 --> 00:02:51.428
Java requires declared types.

49
00:02:51.448 --> 00:02:55.183
Int holds whole numbers; boolean holds true or
false.

50
00:02:55.203 --> 00:02:59.278
The compiler rejects many incompatible assignments
before the game runs.

51
00:02:59.298 --> 00:03:02.528
These named constants make integer values
meaningful.

52
00:03:02.548 --> 00:03:07.348
Static final is the usual pattern for constants
shared by all users of a class.

53
00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:11.485
Luke Fish uses a piece's sign for its color and its
absolute value for its

54
00:03:11.505 --> 00:03:12.640
kind.

55
00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:16.470
A white knight is positive two; a black knight is
negative two.

56
00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:18.100
Zero is empty.

57
00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.860
These are compact conventions, not a special chess
type enforced by Java.

58
00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:25.060
Validation still matters.

59
00:03:25.668 --> 00:03:28.733
The board is a flat array with sixty four slots.

60
00:03:28.753 --> 00:03:31.150
Files and ranks start at zero.

61
00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.385
Multiply the rank by eight, then add the file.

62
00:03:34.405 --> 00:03:38.550
E two has file four and rank one, so its index is
twelve.

63
00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:40.760
E four becomes twenty eight.

64
00:03:41.377 --> 00:03:45.494
Square dot of is a static helper: no Square object
is needed.

65
00:03:45.514 --> 00:03:48.529
Its parameters and return value are integers.

66
00:03:48.549 --> 00:03:52.950
The reverse operations use remainder for the file
and integer division for the rank.

67
00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:56.450
Naming these helpers is clearer than repeating
coordinate arithmetic throughout the

68
00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:58.045
application.

69
00:03:58.668 --> 00:04:03.358
Move is a record containing the source, destination,
promotion, and flags.

70
00:04:03.378 --> 00:04:07.933
Java generates accessors such as from and to, plus
value equality.

71
00:04:07.953 --> 00:04:11.048
The compact constructor checks incoming values.

72
00:04:11.068 --> 00:04:16.020
Each component here is an integer, so callers cannot
mutate the move's contents.

73
00:04:16.627 --> 00:04:21.974
For objects, double equals generally checks whether
two references point to the same object.

74
00:04:21.994 --> 00:04:24.179
Equals can compare values.

75
00:04:24.199 --> 00:04:28.964
Records provide that comparison, which lets a list
recognize an equivalent move.

76
00:04:28.984 --> 00:04:33.546
Flags combine independent facts as bits; the capture
method tests whether the capture bit is

77
00:04:33.566 --> 00:04:34.881
present.

78
00:04:35.502 --> 00:04:37.532
An array has a fixed length.

79
00:04:37.552 --> 00:04:40.222
An Array List can grow as moves are added.

80
00:04:40.242 --> 00:04:45.222
List is the interface, or contract; Array List is
one implementation.

81
00:04:45.242 --> 00:04:48.592
The type inside angle brackets is a generic
parameter.

82
00:04:48.612 --> 00:04:51.707
A list of moves cannot also contain a string.

83
00:04:52.293 --> 00:04:56.353
A final list field cannot be redirected to another
list, but the existing list can

84
00:04:56.373 --> 00:04:57.833
still change.

85
00:04:57.853 --> 00:05:01.993
To protect history, these accessors return List dot
copy Of.

86
00:05:02.013 --> 00:05:07.195
It is an unmodifiable snapshot, though it does not
deeply copy arbitrary elements.

87
00:05:07.215 --> 00:05:11.200
Here, immutable records and boxed numbers make that
useful.

88
00:05:11.793 --> 00:05:13.483
Now follow a pawn.

89
00:05:13.503 --> 00:05:16.448
Direction is the side to move multiplied by eight.

90
00:05:16.468 --> 00:05:19.833
Adding that direction advances one rank in the flat
array.

91
00:05:19.853 --> 00:05:23.392
The if statement checks that the destination exists
and is empty.

92
00:05:23.412 --> 00:05:26.902
A pawn on its starting rank may also advance twice.

93
00:05:27.502 --> 00:05:31.879
The rest of this method handles diagonal captures,
while another helper handles promotion

94
00:05:31.899 --> 00:05:33.174
choices.

95
00:05:33.194 --> 00:05:36.095
Other methods handle knights and sliding pieces.

96
00:05:36.115 --> 00:05:40.862
Loops and conditions are familiar, but named methods
keep each rule understandable.

97
00:05:40.882 --> 00:05:45.087
At this stage, a candidate move may still expose its
own king.

98
00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:49.845
Read this enhanced for loop as: for each move in the
candidate list.

99
00:05:49.865 --> 00:05:55.465
The method remembers which side is moving, applies a
candidate, and checks that side's king.

100
00:05:55.485 --> 00:05:58.982
It then undoes the move before considering the next
candidate.

101
00:05:59.585 --> 00:06:02.360
Only safe candidates enter the legal list.

102
00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:06.145
Notice that the method returns data, not a screen
update.

103
00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:10.095
Testing the resulting position handles pins and
discovered checks without special code for

104
00:06:10.115 --> 00:06:11.905
every possible pin.

105
00:06:11.925 --> 00:06:15.960
Castling also checks that the king does not cross an
attacked square.

106
00:06:16.585 --> 00:06:19.247
Consider this position from the learning guide.

107
00:06:19.267 --> 00:06:23.507
The white pawn on E five appears able to capture the
black pawn en passant.

108
00:06:23.527 --> 00:06:28.447
That special capture moves to D six, while removing
the black pawn from D five.

109
00:06:28.467 --> 00:06:30.912
Two different squares are involved.

110
00:06:31.502 --> 00:06:34.247
But moving the white pawn clears the E file.

111
00:06:34.267 --> 00:06:38.507
The black rook can then attack the white king, so
the capture is illegal.

112
00:06:38.527 --> 00:06:42.582
Make, check, and undo sees the real resulting board.

113
00:06:42.602 --> 00:06:47.232
This is why correct state changes must come before
clever search optimizations.

114
00:06:47.835 --> 00:06:51.940
Position is deliberately mutable: its board changes
in place.

115
00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:56.335
Copying the entire position for every search step
would create much more work.

116
00:06:56.355 --> 00:07:01.340
Instead, make Move returns an Undo record containing
the changed information, and unmake Move

117
00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:02.870
restores it.

118
00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:09.395
Undo must restore castling rights, the en passant
square, move counters, king locations, and

119
00:07:09.415 --> 00:07:12.530
the hash, not merely move a piece backward.

120
00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:15.417
An invariant is a fact that must remain true.

121
00:07:15.437 --> 00:07:20.097
Here, making and unmaking a move must recover
exactly the original state.

122
00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:23.310
Game adds the story around a position.

123
00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:29.517
It validates moves, records notation, stores undo
information, and detects results.

124
00:07:29.537 --> 00:07:34.797
An enum defines a limited set of named
possibilities, such as checkmate and stalemate.

125
00:07:34.817 --> 00:07:39.379
Invalid input throws a typed exception rather than
silently corrupting the board.

126
00:07:40.002 --> 00:07:45.432
F E N is a text format for pieces, turn, castling
rights, en passant, and

127
00:07:45.452 --> 00:07:46.717
counters.

128
00:07:46.737 --> 00:07:49.162
It does not contain earlier moves.

129
00:07:49.182 --> 00:07:52.832
Loading the same F E N does not restore repetition
history.

130
00:07:52.852 --> 00:07:57.147
This classroom app automatically claims threefold
repetition and the fifty move rule;

131
00:07:57.167 --> 00:07:59.392
checkmate takes precedence.

132
00:08:00.002 --> 00:08:02.847
Evaluation assigns a score to a position.

133
00:08:02.867 --> 00:08:08.377
Material values pieces, but Luke Fish also considers
placement, mobility, pawn structure, king

134
00:08:08.397 --> 00:08:11.562
safety, and a small turn bonus called tempo.

135
00:08:11.582 --> 00:08:15.807
The Breakdown record returns these components
together, so the interface can explain the

136
00:08:15.827 --> 00:08:17.132
total.

137
00:08:17.752 --> 00:08:21.642
A knight near useful central squares may receive a
placement bonus.

138
00:08:21.662 --> 00:08:24.262
Isolated pawns can receive a penalty.

139
00:08:24.282 --> 00:08:28.167
These are programmed preferences, not absolute chess
truth.

140
00:08:28.187 --> 00:08:33.697
Pure calculations and small helper methods make an
evaluator easier to understand, change, and

141
00:08:33.717 --> 00:08:36.187
compare against known examples.

142
00:08:36.793 --> 00:08:39.218
The displayed breakdown favors White.

143
00:08:39.238 --> 00:08:43.248
Search, however, wants the current player's
perspective.

144
00:08:43.268 --> 00:08:46.958
Multiplying by the side to move keeps a white score
unchanged and reverses it for

145
00:08:46.978 --> 00:08:48.103
Black.

146
00:08:48.123 --> 00:08:51.993
Clear conventions prevent a positive number from
accidentally meaning opposite things in

147
00:08:52.013 --> 00:08:53.738
different places.

148
00:08:54.335 --> 00:08:58.935
Evaluation also blends middlegame and endgame values
using a phase weight.

149
00:08:58.955 --> 00:09:02.210
A king often wants shelter early and activity late.

150
00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:07.275
Rather than switch abruptly, the blend changes
gradually as material disappears.

151
00:09:07.295 --> 00:09:12.785
The helper returns an integer, so Java's integer
division discards any fractional remainder.

152
00:09:13.377 --> 00:09:16.392
The Engine lab creates an immutable settings record.

153
00:09:16.412 --> 00:09:19.357
Its constructor validates ranges immediately.

154
00:09:19.377 --> 00:09:23.652
A search gets one snapshot instead of repeatedly
reading live controls.

155
00:09:23.672 --> 00:09:27.737
That makes its behavior easier to reason about, even
if the user changes a spinner

156
00:09:27.757 --> 00:09:29.522
while it is thinking.

157
00:09:30.127 --> 00:09:32.777
Depth controls the main search horizon.

158
00:09:32.797 --> 00:09:34.932
Time limits the thinking budget.

159
00:09:34.952 --> 00:09:37.612
Weights adjust evaluation preferences.

160
00:09:37.632 --> 00:09:41.717
Randomness affects root move selection, not
recursive scores.

161
00:09:41.737 --> 00:09:47.059
For a fair experiment, set randomness to zero, hold
the position fixed, and change just

162
00:09:47.079 --> 00:09:49.040
one setting at a time.

163
00:09:49.627 --> 00:09:54.169
Search asks a different question from evaluation:
what could happen next?

164
00:09:54.189 --> 00:09:56.519
Imagine White has moves A and B.

165
00:09:56.539 --> 00:10:01.075
After A, Black can allow a score of plus thirty or
plus eighty for White.

166
00:10:01.095 --> 00:10:05.270
A sensible opponent chooses the smaller advantage,
plus thirty.

167
00:10:05.877 --> 00:10:09.607
After B, suppose Black can hold White to plus ten.

168
00:10:09.627 --> 00:10:12.687
White prefers A's guaranteed thirty to B's ten.

169
00:10:12.707 --> 00:10:16.452
These are illustrative numbers, not a live engine
result.

170
00:10:16.472 --> 00:10:18.502
One player's move is a ply.

171
00:10:18.522 --> 00:10:22.407
Recursion repeats this decision process at each
level of the tree.

172
00:10:23.002 --> 00:10:26.342
A recursive method calls itself on a smaller
problem.

173
00:10:26.362 --> 00:10:29.689
Each call has its own local variables on the call
stack.

174
00:10:29.709 --> 00:10:32.954
Here, depth decreases and ply increases.

175
00:10:32.974 --> 00:10:38.574
When depth reaches zero, the engine switches to
quiescence, which we will explain shortly.

176
00:10:38.594 --> 00:10:42.044
Checkmate and stalemate are also stopping
conditions.

177
00:10:42.668 --> 00:10:46.143
Negamax evaluates from the player to move's
perspective.

178
00:10:46.163 --> 00:10:51.162
After our move, it is the opponent's turn, so the
returned score is negated.

179
00:10:51.182 --> 00:10:53.657
Good for the opponent means bad for us.

180
00:10:53.677 --> 00:10:58.091
The alpha and beta arguments also reverse signs and
order; they describe the useful search

181
00:10:58.111 --> 00:10:59.356
window.

182
00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:05.455
Instead of starting at the maximum depth, iterative
deepening finishes shallow searches first.

183
00:11:05.475 --> 00:11:08.515
Each completed iteration becomes the current answer.

184
00:11:08.535 --> 00:11:12.679
It also suggests a promising move to try first in
the next iteration, helping the

185
00:11:12.699 --> 00:11:15.174
deeper search work more efficiently.

186
00:11:15.793 --> 00:11:20.118
If the time budget expires halfway through a depth,
the engine keeps the last completed

187
00:11:20.138 --> 00:11:21.368
result.

188
00:11:21.388 --> 00:11:25.223
If no depth finishes, it still provides a legal
fallback.

189
00:11:25.243 --> 00:11:29.641
The budget is soft because stop requests are checked
at selected points, not enforced by

190
00:11:29.661 --> 00:11:32.481
interrupting every individual operation.

191
00:11:33.085 --> 00:11:36.825
Search temporarily changes the position and
repetition history.

192
00:11:36.845 --> 00:11:41.153
If a stop request throws an exception deep in
recursion, ordinary statements after the

193
00:11:41.173 --> 00:11:43.389
recursive call might never run.

194
00:11:43.409 --> 00:11:47.339
A finally block performs cleanup while the call
stack unwinds.

195
00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:51.775
Here, cleanup pops the history entry and unmakes the
move.

196
00:11:51.795 --> 00:11:56.505
This is a general Java pattern: pair an operation
with guaranteed cleanup, including

197
00:11:56.525 --> 00:11:58.295
exceptional exits.

198
00:11:58.315 --> 00:12:02.230
Expected cancellation is handled separately from
real failures.

199
00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:07.020
Do not hide every exception; unexpected problems
need visible reporting.

200
00:12:07.627 --> 00:12:09.657
Return to our small tree.

201
00:12:09.677 --> 00:12:12.492
White already has an option worth plus thirty.

202
00:12:12.512 --> 00:12:16.647
Under move B, we discover that Black can hold White
to plus ten.

203
00:12:16.667 --> 00:12:19.827
We do not need to examine every other Black reply to
know that B cannot

204
00:12:19.847 --> 00:12:21.882
improve White's decision.

205
00:12:22.502 --> 00:12:25.492
Alpha and beta express these useful bounds.

206
00:12:25.512 --> 00:12:29.362
When the window closes, the code stops searching
that branch.

207
00:12:29.382 --> 00:12:31.417
This is not random guessing.

208
00:12:31.437 --> 00:12:36.127
Given the same depth and evaluation rules, correct
pruning preserves the score.

209
00:12:36.147 --> 00:12:40.209
Better move ordering often lets the engine discover
these cutoffs sooner.

210
00:12:40.835 --> 00:12:44.155
This lambda takes a move and produces an ordering
score.

211
00:12:44.175 --> 00:12:49.495
The comparator sorts moves by that score, reversed
so promising candidates come first.

212
00:12:49.515 --> 00:12:54.060
Lambdas let Java pass behavior into another method,
much like an arrow function used as

213
00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:56.282
a callback in JavaScript.

214
00:12:56.877 --> 00:13:02.045
Luke Fish prioritizes a preferred move, promotions,
and captures, then uses remembered cutoff

215
00:13:02.065 --> 00:13:04.239
moves and a history heuristic.

216
00:13:04.259 --> 00:13:09.354
It also uses principal variation search: probe later
moves with a narrow window, then search

217
00:13:09.374 --> 00:13:11.084
more fully if needed.

218
00:13:11.104 --> 00:13:15.149
These optimize searching; they do not redefine legal
chess.

219
00:13:15.752 --> 00:13:20.872
Imagine evaluating immediately after winning a
piece, before the opponent recaptures.

220
00:13:20.892 --> 00:13:23.452
The position can look misleadingly good.

221
00:13:23.472 --> 00:13:28.034
Quiescence follows captures and promotions beyond
the ordinary depth limit, subject to its

222
00:13:28.054 --> 00:13:31.644
configured extension budget and the engine's safety
limits.

223
00:13:32.252 --> 00:13:37.462
When not in check, the current evaluation can serve
as a baseline called standing pat.

224
00:13:37.482 --> 00:13:40.337
In check, doing nothing is not legal.

225
00:13:40.357 --> 00:13:45.277
The engine must consider evasions, including quiet
moves, even when the capture extension is

226
00:13:45.297 --> 00:13:46.767
set to zero.

227
00:13:46.787 --> 00:13:50.442
Terminal positions are checked before trusting an
evaluation.

228
00:13:51.043 --> 00:13:54.028
Different move orders can lead to the same position.

229
00:13:54.048 --> 00:13:58.843
To recognize positions quickly, Luke Fish builds a
sixty four bit fingerprint called a Zobrist

230
00:13:58.863 --> 00:13:59.838
key.

231
00:13:59.858 --> 00:14:01.953
Java's long type holds it.

232
00:14:01.973 --> 00:14:06.813
Bitwise exclusive or combines piece and square keys;
applying the same key twice cancels that

233
00:14:06.833 --> 00:14:08.458
contribution.

234
00:14:09.043 --> 00:14:13.463
The full repetition key also includes the turn,
castling rights, and legally available en

235
00:14:13.483 --> 00:14:14.688
passant.

236
00:14:14.708 --> 00:14:17.873
Move counters are not part of repetition identity.

237
00:14:17.893 --> 00:14:21.688
Hashing is fast, but not mathematically collision
free.

238
00:14:21.708 --> 00:14:26.938
This is a compact lookup tool, not a perfect proof
that two positions are identical.

239
00:14:27.543 --> 00:14:31.798
The transposition table stores previous search
information in a bounded array.

240
00:14:31.818 --> 00:14:36.823
Its entries include depth, score, a bound type, and
the best move.

241
00:14:36.843 --> 00:14:40.958
A cutoff may prove only a lower or upper bound, so
treating every stored number

242
00:14:40.978 --> 00:14:43.213
as exact would be incorrect.

243
00:14:43.835 --> 00:14:48.152
The engine also checks the halfmove clock and a
repetition history fingerprint before reusing

244
00:14:48.172 --> 00:14:49.347
a score.

245
00:14:49.367 --> 00:14:52.337
Identical boards can have different draw futures.

246
00:14:52.357 --> 00:14:56.179
A remembered move can still help ordering when that
context differs.

247
00:14:56.199 --> 00:15:01.216
The lesson is broader than chess: cache validity
depends on all relevant inputs.

248
00:15:01.835 --> 00:15:06.700
Board Panel extends Swing's J Panel, reusing the
component framework and specializing painting

249
00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:08.035
and input.

250
00:15:08.055 --> 00:15:13.175
It reports requested squares through a callback; it
cannot independently play a move.

251
00:15:13.195 --> 00:15:17.220
The method reference with two colons passes Chess
Frame's request Board Move method as that

252
00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:18.675
callback.

253
00:15:19.293 --> 00:15:22.803
The coordinate text field uses an action listener
lambda.

254
00:15:22.823 --> 00:15:27.203
It asks the model for a legal move, then asks the
controller to play it.

255
00:15:27.223 --> 00:15:30.328
An invalid move becomes a helpful input message.

256
00:15:30.348 --> 00:15:34.715
Painting, input handling, and rule validation remain
separate even though the player

257
00:15:34.735 --> 00:15:36.920
experiences one action.

258
00:15:37.543 --> 00:15:41.518
Swing has an event dispatch thread, often called the
E D T.

259
00:15:41.538 --> 00:15:45.283
If a long search ran there, clicks and painting
would freeze.

260
00:15:45.303 --> 00:15:48.108
Chess Frame starts a Swing Worker instead.

261
00:15:48.128 --> 00:15:53.053
The worker searches a copied position using
immutable settings and a history snapshot.

262
00:15:53.668 --> 00:15:57.438
Do in Background performs computation on a
background thread.

263
00:15:57.458 --> 00:16:02.688
Published progress reaches process on the event
thread, and done handles completion there too.

264
00:16:02.708 --> 00:16:05.876
The UI and search do not mutate the same position.

265
00:16:05.896 --> 00:16:10.811
Ownership and message passing are often simpler than
putting locks around every operation.

266
00:16:11.418 --> 00:16:14.273
Stopping a worker is not enough by itself.

267
00:16:14.293 --> 00:16:18.405
A result may already be finishing when the user
chooses Undo or New game.

268
00:16:18.425 --> 00:16:20.992
Chess Frame increments a revision number.

269
00:16:21.012 --> 00:16:24.974
A worker remembers its starting revision and rejects
completion if that revision is no longer

270
00:16:24.994 --> 00:16:26.269
current.

271
00:16:26.877 --> 00:16:30.962
Atomic Boolean carries the cooperative stop request
between threads.

272
00:16:30.982 --> 00:16:34.444
Move now sets that signal but preserves the usable
result.

273
00:16:34.464 --> 00:16:37.499
Undo also cancels and invalidates the worker.

274
00:16:37.519 --> 00:16:42.049
These are intentionally different behaviors: one
wants the best completed move; the other

275
00:16:42.069 --> 00:16:44.684
wants no old move applied at all.

276
00:16:45.293 --> 00:16:47.173
Put the pieces together.

277
00:16:47.193 --> 00:16:49.868
The player enters E two, E four.

278
00:16:49.888 --> 00:16:52.548
Square helpers interpret coordinates.

279
00:16:52.568 --> 00:16:55.745
Legal move generation finds the matching Move
record.

280
00:16:55.765 --> 00:16:58.875
Game applies it and updates history and results.

281
00:16:58.895 --> 00:17:03.325
Chess Frame refreshes the display and decides
whether the computer should respond.

282
00:17:03.918 --> 00:17:06.323
A copied position enters search.

283
00:17:06.343 --> 00:17:11.678
Recursive exploration calls evaluation, uses
ordering and caching, and eventually returns a

284
00:17:11.698 --> 00:17:13.478
Search Result record.

285
00:17:13.498 --> 00:17:17.373
Back on the event thread, the revision check
protects the live game before the result

286
00:17:17.393 --> 00:17:18.653
is played.

287
00:17:18.673 --> 00:17:22.568
Each boundary has a clear Java type and a clear
responsibility.

288
00:17:23.168 --> 00:17:27.773
Perft counts legal move sequences without search
pruning or draw adjudication.

289
00:17:27.793 --> 00:17:32.748
From the starting position, the first four counts
are twenty, four hundred, eight thousand

290
00:17:32.768 --> 00:17:37.668
nine hundred two, and one hundred ninety seven
thousand two hundred eighty one.

291
00:17:37.688 --> 00:17:40.958
Those precise numbers reveal subtle rule mistakes.

292
00:17:41.543 --> 00:17:47.006
Other tests verify undo restoration, position
hashes, special moves, and agreement with a

293
00:17:47.026 --> 00:17:49.001
simpler reference search.

294
00:17:49.021 --> 00:17:51.836
Think of a test as an executable promise.

295
00:17:51.856 --> 00:17:56.416
If move counts change unexpectedly, inspect legality
and make or unmake before adjusting

296
00:17:56.436 --> 00:17:58.241
evaluation weights.

297
00:17:58.261 --> 00:18:01.748
A stronger sounding preference cannot fix a broken
rule.

298
00:18:02.335 --> 00:18:05.665
Set a breakpoint in Square dot parse and enter a
move.

299
00:18:05.685 --> 00:18:08.787
Inspect the input string and resulting indices.

300
00:18:08.807 --> 00:18:12.892
Next, stop in make Move and compare the board before
and after.

301
00:18:12.912 --> 00:18:18.637
In Eclipse, F five steps in, F six steps over, and F
eight resumes.

302
00:18:19.252 --> 00:18:22.792
Use a conditional breakpoint to focus on one source
square.

303
00:18:22.812 --> 00:18:27.082
Search time still passes while the debugger is
paused, so start shallow and allow a

304
00:18:27.102 --> 00:18:28.607
larger budget.

305
00:18:28.627 --> 00:18:31.425
Model tests are better for leisurely stepping.

306
00:18:31.445 --> 00:18:36.595
Read the first compiler error first; later errors
may simply be consequences.

307
00:18:37.210 --> 00:18:42.385
Try this experiment: analyze the same position with
move ordering enabled and disabled.

308
00:18:42.405 --> 00:18:48.315
Keep depth, evaluation, and quiescence fixed, with
randomness zero and enough time to finish.

309
00:18:48.335 --> 00:18:53.545
At the same completed depth, scores should agree,
though tied best moves can differ.

310
00:18:53.565 --> 00:18:57.075
Compare visited nodes, not just elapsed time.

311
00:18:57.668 --> 00:19:01.038
Then make one small code change and add a focused
check.

312
00:19:01.058 --> 00:19:07.870
You have now seen types, objects, records,
collections, loops, recursion, exceptions,

313
00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:11.055
callbacks, and concurrency working together.

314
00:19:11.075 --> 00:19:15.245
Luke Fish is a classical educational engine, not
Stockfish.

315
00:19:15.265 --> 00:19:19.612
Its greatest feature is that you can understand and
change the system yourself.
